===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The act of assuring; a declaration tending to inspire full confidence; that which is designed to give confidence.
#* {{w|Acts of the Apostles|Acts}} xvii. 31.
#*: Whereof he hath given '''assurance''' unto all men, in that he hath raised him from the dead.
#* {{rfdate|and other bibliographic particulars}} {{w|Thomas Babington Macaulay, 1st Baron Macaulay|Thomas Macaulay}}
#*: '''Assurances''' of support came pouring in daily.
# The state of being assured; firm persuasion; full confidence or trust; freedom from doubt; certainty.
#* {{w|Epistle to the Hebrews|Hebrews}} x. 22.
#*: Let us draw with a true heart in full '''assurance''' of faith, having our hearts sprinkled from an evil conscience.
# Firmness of mind; undoubting, steadiness; intrepidity; courage; confidence; self-reliance.
#* {{rfdate|and other bibliographic particulars}} {{w|Richard Knolles}}
#*: Brave men meet danger with '''assurance'''.
#* {{rfdate|and other bibliographic particulars}} {{w|John Locke}}
#*: Conversation with the world will give them knowledge and '''assurance'''.
#* {{RQ:EHough PrqsPrc|I|0108}}
#*: This new-comer was a man who in any company would have seemed striking.{{...}}His air, of self-confident '''assurance''', seemed that of a man well used to having his own way.
# Excess of boldness; impudence; audacity
#: ''his '''assurance''' is intolerable''
#* {{quote-book| year=1815| author={{w|Jane Austen}}| title={{w|Emma}}| volume=I| section=chapter 7| passage=''You'' confined to the society of the illiterate and vulgar all your life! I wonder how the young man could have the '''assurance''' to ask it. He must have a pretty good opinion of himself.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Betrothal; affiance.
# {{lb|en|insurance}} [[insurance|Insurance]]; a contract for the payment of a sum on occasion of a certain event, as loss or death. Recently, assurance has been used, in England, in relation to life contingencies, and insurance in relation to other contingencies. It is called temporary assurance, in the time within which the contingent event must happen is limited.
# {{lb|en|legal}} Any written or other legal evidence of the conveyance of property; a conveyance; a deed.
#* ''c''. '''1766''', {{w|William Blackstone}}, {{w|Commentaries on the Laws of England}}
#*: the legal evidences of the conveyance of property are called the common '''assurances''' of the kingdom.
# {{lb|en|theology}} Subjective certainty of one's [[salvation]].

